    id: "leads-client-date-hydration-mismatch",
    decision:
      "Replaced all 13 remaining raw new Date(x).toLocaleDateString() / .toLocaleString() call sites in src/app/dashboard/leads/leads-client.tsx with the already-established, already-tested formatAbandonedAgeHours(deployHandoffAgeHours(x, nowMs)) + \" ago\" pattern (pure, Date.parse-based, locale/timezone-independent) — the same fix this file's own history already applied once, to the 'Bundle order refunds & disputes' panel, but never carried through to the other 13 instances of the identical bug class.",
    rationale:
      "leads-client.tsx is a \"use client\" component, but Next.js still server-renders every \"use client\" component for the initial HTML before hydrating it in the browser — \"use client\" only marks a component as hydratable, it does not opt it out of SSR. Date#toLocaleDateString()/toLocaleString() called with no explicit timeZone resolve against the *runtime's* system timezone, and the SSR pass runs on the Node.js server (in production, whatever the deployment host's timezone is — typically UTC) while the hydration pass runs in the operator's own browser (whatever their local timezone is). Confirmed this is a genuine, non-theoretical divergence, not just a formatting nit: TZ=UTC node -e \"console.log(new Date('2026-07-19T02:30:00.000Z').toLocaleDateString())\" prints 7/19/2026 while TZ=America/Los_Angeles on the identical ISO timestamp prints 7/18/2026 — a full calendar-day difference, not merely a locale string variation. Since none of the 13 call sites had suppressHydrationWarning or a mount-guard, a real deployment (server timezone almost never equal to a distributed operator's browser timezone) would show the wrong calendar date on these operator-facing money/CRM timestamps (abandoned-checkout-session start time, last recovery nudge, Commerce order/payout dates, inquiry received/replied time, last-outreach/last-preview-open dates) on first paint, then silently swap to a different value once hydration completes — exactly the class of thing that erodes operator trust in dashboard numbers, and normally also throws a hydration-mismatch console error in dev.\n\nThis is not a manufactured metric: this exact bug class (raw toLocaleDateString() calendar-date rendering on a client-hydrated dashboard panel) was already identified and fixed once before in this same file, for the 'Bundle order refunds & disputes' panel (see the oldestBundleOrderRefundOrDisputeAgeHours / deployHandoffAgeHours entries earlier in this log) — that fix explicitly replaced 'a raw toLocaleDateString() calendar date' with an 'Xh ago' duration render using the same two pure helpers this entry reuses. The other 13 call sites in the same file were simply never swept for the identical pattern. Two of the 13 sites made the duplication especially visible on read: the Commerce-payouts-owed row rendered row.createdAt via raw toLocaleDateString() on one line and, three lines later in the exact same <li>, rendered the identical concept via the already-safe formatAbandonedAgeHours(deployHandoffAgeHours(row.createdAt, nowMs)) pattern for the sibling 'owed' line; the Website-inquiries panel likewise rendered inq.createdAt via raw toLocaleString() unconditionally, then five lines later computed deployHandoffAgeHours(inq.createdAt, nowMs) on the very same field only for the conditional 'received Xh ago' badge on unreplied inquiries.\n\nAlso independently ruled out, before settling on this finding, the specific 'apiSecret migration straggler' hypothesis raised as the most-promising remaining lead: scripted a regex count of every api.<table>.<fn> call site in src/lib/db.ts (67) against every apiSecret: convexApiSecret occurrence in the same file (67, exact match) and, separately, every exported query/mutation in all 10 migrated convex/*.ts files against its requireApiSecret( call count (exact match in every file) — the migration series is fully clean with no stragglers, so did not pursue it further.\n\nApplied the fix uniformly: reused an already-in-scope, already-computed age variable where one existed (sessionAgeHours for the two abandoned-checkout-session 'started' lines, dropping the now-fully-redundant separate '· due Xh ago' clause those two lines previously carried alongside the raw date), and computed deployHandoffAgeHours(field, nowMs) inline everywhere else (commerce-payouts summary 'latest' line, commerce order row createdAt/paidOutAt, follow-ups-due and cold-leads 'last sent' lines, inquiry repliedAt and the always-shown createdAt line, and the leads-table lastOutreachAt/lastPreviewOpenAt columns) — the same deployHandoffAgeHours/formatAbandonedAgeHours pair already imported and unit-tested in src/lib/metrics/pipeline-health.test.ts, so no new pure logic needed new coverage (matching this log's established precedent for pure-JSX-wiring iterations that reuse already-tested helpers, e.g. dashboard-landing-staleness-signal). For the Website-inquiries panel's always-shown createdAt line, simplified away the now-literally-redundant nested 'X ago · received X ago' wording (both computed off the same inq.createdAt) into a single 'X ago' plus a plain '· unreplied' status word for unreplied inquiries, rather than leaving the duplicate phrasing intact.\n\nVerified far more thoroughly than a curl-for-200 pass, since this bug class is specifically invisible to that check (the SSR HTML always looks superficially fine in isolation — the bug only shows up as a *mismatch* between two renders under different timezones): grepped src/app/dashboard/leads/leads-client.tsx for toLocaleDateString/toLocaleString/toLocaleTimeString post-fix (zero remaining matches) and confirmed leads-client.tsx was the only file in src/ with this pattern (grepped the whole tree for the same methods, no other .ts/.tsx hits). Then, with npm run dev running, inserted real rows directly into local.db via node:sqlite (a lead with lastOutreachAt/lastPreviewOpenAt; an open bundle checkout_sessions row with createdAt 9h old and recoverySentAt 3 days old, past the recovery cooldown so it actually appears in the Abandoned-checkouts due queue; a commerce_orders row; a replied and a separate unreplied client_inquiries row) and curled /dashboard/leads repeatedly across each edit, confirming the exact expected computed values rendered in the live SSR HTML at every one of the 13 sites: 'started 9h ago · last nudged 3d ago', '· latest 1d 6h ago', '$42 · Widget · 1d 6h ago' beside the pre-existing 'owed 1d 6h ago' sibling line, 'Your reply · 4h ago', '7h ago · unreplied', and the leads-table row showing '5h ago' / 'Preview 2h ago' exactly matching the inserted lastOutreachAt/lastPreviewOpenAt timestamps — then deleted every inserted test row and confirmed /dashboard/leads, /dashboard, /preview/demo-cafe, /docs/process all still returned 200 with zero error-boundary markers before stopping the dev server and confirming port 3000 free. Verified: npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json (0 errors) + npm run lint (clean) + npm test (2303 passed, 174 files, unchanged — pure JSX wiring reusing already-tested pure helpers, no new logic branching to cover) + npm run build (clean, same 34-route ○/ƒ split).",
